PyTorch (ROCm/pytorch release/2.8, commit d618db8): number warps by the
hardware wavefront in the binary prefix scan that torch.topk uses.

WHY
torch.topk on gfx803 hangs the GPU, and the driver then resets it ("SQ
instruction intr detected ... trap true", then "qcm fence wait loop timeout",
"Failed to evict process queues", BACO reset, "VRAM is lost"). Any sampler
that applies top-k or top-p on the GPU triggers it: Chatterbox TTS's
token sampling did on the first generated token. With
AMD_SERIALIZE_KERNEL=3 the HIP runtime names the kernel
(at::native::sbtopk::gatherTopK<float, unsigned int, 1, false>, slice
size 8194) and reports HSA_STATUS_ERROR_EXCEPTION before the reset.

gatherTopK finds each kept element's output slot with
at::cuda::exclusiveBinaryPrefixScan (aten/src/ATen/cuda/ScanUtils.cuh). On
ROCm the scan votes with a 64-bit ballot over the whole hardware wavefront
and has lane 0 store the wavefront's count, but it numbers warps as
threadIdx.x / C10_WARP_SIZE. c10/macros/Macros.h makes C10_WARP_SIZE 64 in
gfx9 device code and 32 for every other AMD target, so on gfx803's 64-lane
wavefronts lanes 32-63 form a "warp" whose count nobody writes: thread 0
sums uninitialized shared memory into the offsets, the slots come out
wrong, and CUDA_KERNEL_ASSERT(writeIndex < outputSliceSize) executes
s_trap. On gfx803 the trapped wave never ends, so the queue cannot be
preempted and the driver resets the GPU instead of the process getting an
error. Where the assert does not fire, the top-k values are silently
wrong.

To reproduce without the trap:
tools/correctness-suite/pytorch/scan_repro.hip runs the scan as written in
random 64-1024 thread blocks; with a warp width of 32 every block (100 of
100) gets wrong slots and totals, with the hardware's warpSize none.

Changing C10_WARP_SIZE itself for gfx8 is not an option: other kernels
depend on its value of 32 there (see gfx803-layer-norm-warp-size.patch:
group_norm's gradients break). The fix stays in the scan.

WHAT
binaryScanWarpSize() returns warpSize (the hardware wavefront) on ROCm
and C10_WARP_SIZE on CUDA; the scan's three warp computations use it.
CUDA builds and gfx9 (where the two agree) are unchanged. The other
ballot users were checked: SortingRadixSelect.cuh's countRadixUsingMask
already counts per hardware wavefront (lane 0 of the ballot, __popcll),
and Embedding.cu's backward kernel takes its block width from the host's
warp size (64), consistent with the ballot.

REAL-HARDWARE VERIFICATION (RX 590, this image)
tools/correctness-suite/pytorch/topk_check.py: torch.topk matches the CPU
in 798 of 798 cases (sizes 64 to 1M including 8194- and 151936-entry
vocabularies, fp32, fp16 and int64, k from 1 to the full size, largest and
smallest, sorted and unsorted, masked logits and all-equal inputs), and
Hugging Face's top-k, top-p and min-p filters keep the same tokens as on
the CPU in 150 of 150 trials. Chatterbox TTS, whose sampler trapped on its
first token, generates speech with no trap or reset. PyTorch op suite 118
of 118, SD 1.5 UNet check stages 1 and 2, conv checks fp32 and fp16 0 bad,
20 LoRA training steps (median 4 s/step, loss 0.219), 0 GPU faults.

